# ripgrep configuration.
#
# ripgrep reads this file only when RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH points at it, which is why the
# shell configurations export it. A config file with nothing exporting the variable is the
# quiet failure mode here: rg keeps working, at its defaults, and nothing says why.
#
# Deliberately small. rg's defaults are good, and every option here changes behaviour that
# was actually noticed rather than one that reads well in a blog post.

# Case-insensitive until the pattern contains a capital, matching the search behaviour
# already configured for neovim, fzf and less on this machine.
--smart-case

# Follow symlinks. ~/.config is full of them on a chezmoi-managed machine, and a search
# that silently skips them is worse than one that is slightly slower.
--follow

# Long lines are usually minified assets or a base64 blob, and printing one fills the
# terminal without telling you anything. The match is still reported, just truncated.
--max-columns=200
--max-columns-preview

# Hidden files are searched, because dotfiles are the point of this repository. .git is
# still excluded by the default ignore rules, so this does not mean walking object storage.
--hidden
--glob=!.git/
